# A tree data structure is a hierarchical  structure that is used to represent and organize data in a way that is easy to navigate and search. It is a collection of nodes that are connected by edges and has a hierarchical relationship between the node. it looks like a upside down tree consisting of nodes.

# Basic terminologies in trees 
# 1. root node : every tree has one main node called a root. The root represents the starting node of all tree types.
# 2. child nodes and parent node: from the root, all lower nodes attached to a higher node is called a child node. All higher nodes, which generates lower nodes are called parent nodes. 
# 3. sibling nodes : two nodes are siblings is they descend directly from the same parent. 
# 4. leaf node : nodes that have no children are called leaf nodes. the term leaf node comes from the leaves of a tree, as they represent end of the branch
# 5. subtree : from the root, each new child represents a subtree, a new tree recursively resets itself.
# 6. level of a tree : the level defines the depth of a tree, so that the children of a node is always placed at later levels.
# 7. height of a tree: it represents the largest depth of a tree.  represents the distance between the root and the leaf node of the highest level of the tree 

# types of trees
# 1. bianary tree : in a bianary tree each node can have a maximum of two children linked to it
# 2. ternary tree : a ternary tree is a tree data sturuce in which each node has at most three child nodes, usually distinguished as left, mid, right
# 3. N-ary tree or generic tree: generic trees are a collection of nodes where each nodes consists of list of preferances to its children 